{
  "id": "koko-eating-bananas",
  "questionId": "koko-eating-bananas",
  "slug": "koko-eating-bananas",
  "title": "Koko Eating Bananas",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Binary search",
  "patterns": [
    "binary search",
    "binary search on answer"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "you're not searching the piles array itself - you're searching over possible eating speeds. what makes a speed 'valid'?",
    "as speed k increases, the hours needed to finish all piles only ever decreases or stays the same - that monotonic relationship is what makes binary search possible here",
    "for a candidate speed k, computing the hours needed just means summing ceil(pile / k) over every pile",
    "binary search k between 1 and the largest pile (no speed faster than the biggest pile ever helps); for each candidate, check if hours(k) <= h, and shrink toward the smallest k that still works"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "trying to binary search directly over the piles array instead of over the space of possible speeds",
    "using integer division instead of ceiling division when computing hours for a pile (e.g. pile=7, k=4 needs 2 hours, not 1)",
    "setting the upper bound of the speed search too low (must allow at least the maximum pile size, since Koko can't split a pile across hours in a way that requires more)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Koko has piles of bananas to eat before the guards return in `h` hours.\nEach hour, she picks one pile and eats up to `k` bananas from it, where\n`k` is her eating speed (a fixed integer she chooses in advance). If the\npile has fewer than `k` bananas left, she finishes that pile for the\nhour and doesn't carry the leftover speed over to another pile that\nsame hour.\n\n**Given the piles and `h`, return the minimum integer eating speed `k`\nthat lets her finish every pile within `h` hours.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= piles.length <= 10^4",
      "piles.length <= h <= 10^9",
      "1 <= piles[i] <= 10^9"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "h exactly equals the number of piles (forces speed to cover the largest pile)",
      "h is very large relative to the piles (speed 1 might already work)",
      "a single pile"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "piles = [3, 6, 7, 11], h = 8",
        "output": "4",
        "expectedOutput": "4",
        "explanation": "at speed 4, the hours needed are ceil(3/4)+ceil(6/4)+ceil(7/4)+ceil(11/4) = 1+2+2+3 = 8, exactly fitting"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "piles = [30, 11, 23, 4, 20], h = 5",
        "output": "30",
        "expectedOutput": "30",
        "explanation": "with only 5 hours for 5 piles, she must clear each pile in a single hour, so speed must cover the largest pile"
      },
